  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Friday)\nIssued at 1158 AM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nTemperatures are running a few degrees cooler than 24 hours\nthanks to cloud coverage stemming from ongoing convection in\nOK/KS. A few showers ahead of the main cluster of storms are\ntraversing across the Ozarks as of 11 am. Confidence in these\nshowers affecting the Mid-South is low due to the presence of\nupper level ridging resulting in large dewpoint depression\nvalues. Sunday will generally be a rinse and repeat of today as\nthe synoptic pattern remains stagnant. Upper level moisture will\nbegin to increase on Sunday and more on Monday as Hurricane Polo\nis forecast to make landfall on the Baja California Peninsula.\nThis moisture, however, will take time to dampen the current\n586 dam ridge over the area, so the largest effects will not be\nfelt until southerly flow overtakes the entire column by Tuesday\nand temperatures and humidity climb.\n\nThe latest deterministic and ensemble guidance has favored a\nslower evolution of a cut off low developing in the Desert\nSouthwest before traversing northeast across the CONUS. This\nunfortunately delays the cold front associated with this system\nand accompanied rainfall. Most guidance does not bring widespread\nPoPs to the forecast until Thursday evening at the earliest.\nThere are still large discrepancies in the timing of FROPA, noted\nby 10-15 degree temperature differences between the NBM 25th and\n75th percentiles late next week. Enjoy the last days of cooler\nconditions before summer like humidity returns by midweek.\n\nDNM",
